# fragments/accel/rk3576-npu.config -- RK3576 NPU (rocket) enablement.
#
# Turns on the mainline in-tree `rocket` DRM-accel driver and the Rockchip IOMMU
# it binds through. Named by a board whose `.dts` enables the NPU nodes (today the
# H96 MAX M9), so an RK3576 board without that enable keeps a lean kernel.
#
# The rocket Kconfig (drivers/accel/rocket/Kconfig) gates on DRM_ACCEL,
# ROCKCHIP_IOMMU, ARCH_ROCKCHIP && ARM64, and MMU; the first two are not otherwise
# pinned for RK3576, so name them here. The RK3576 support itself comes from the
# rk3576-npu patch series (the driver's of_device_id match data + the NPU DT
# nodes); this fragment only compiles the driver in.
#
# Binding the driver exposes the accel uAPI (/dev/accel), and jobs encoded for the
# RK3576 run on it: convolutions are bit-exact against a CPU model on this silicon,
# back to back. The register program is SoC-specific -- the RK3576 map is shifted and
# re-packed relative to the RK3588 -- so a userspace has to carry an RK3576 encoder,
# and no released one does yet (rocket-userspace is bit-exact on the RK3588 and names
# the RK3576 as its next target). Nothing in Debian opens an accel node either, so this
# fragment supplies a device, not a working stack.
CONFIG_DRM_ACCEL=y
CONFIG_ROCKCHIP_IOMMU=y
CONFIG_DRM_ACCEL_ROCKET=m
